{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '404,422p' docs/design/yang.wasm.md; sed -n '1080,1138p' docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md; sed -n '1438,1475p' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md; rg -n '\"'^'\"\\\\| \"'`:return rs`'\"' docs/design/yang.wasm.md; tail -8 collab/1792349357932-architect-wasm-spec-tree-fidelity.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"## 4. Target interface specification: Import ABI `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`\n\n*(Owner amendment note, 2026-10-19: By explicit owner ruling, the service import ABI is named\n`yin.vm.engine.host.v1` — renamed from `yin.register.host.v1` in r5 §4.1.1 — and its canonical\nprotocol tables live in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3. This section specifies the\ncompiler's target-interface binding and references those tables rather than duplicating them.)*\n\nEmitted WebAssembly modules target the guest-agnostic engine service protocol\n**`yin.vm.engine.host.v1`** defined in [`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3 (r5 §4.1.1):\n\n1. **Module import bindings ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3):** The shared runtime module imports `memory` (one wasm32, non-shared memory), `functions` (one original function table), and **`exchange(control_ptr: i32) -> i32`** from module name `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`. Generated component modules import the same `memory`/`functions` and the service entry as needed, plus declared immutable `i32` base-offset words. No import or export passes a JS value, `externref`, Rust pointer, string object, multi-value result, or `i64` through a host-call signature.\n2. **Control-block layout and buffer ownership ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.1):** Emitted service thunks address ABI control exactly as §E.3.1 defines — the control-block field/offset map, magic, version fields, header length, request/response buffer boundaries and task-allocation start are owned by that section, and the compiler must emit offsets consistent with it. Guest allocation/growth occurs between `exchange` calls, never inside `exchange`.\n3. **Transport statuses, execution statuses, and `fetch-response` ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.2):** Emitted service thunks handle the transport-status set and the response-retry rule exactly as §E.3.2 defines: on `NEED_SPACE` the guest thunk resizes the response buffer to the required byte length and issues a `fetch-response` for the same sequence number without re-executing or re-sending the semantic transition. Decoded responses carry the private scheduling status set of §E.3.2.\n4. **TLV framing, expanded-canonical-bytes ordering, and Float64/NaN encoding ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.3):** All request/response payloads and constant/value definitions emitted or decoded by the runtime module obey §E.3.3's single outer framing rule, its expanded-canonical-bytes ordering rule (map keys and set elements sorted before tag-`13` handle compression), and its Float64/NaN canonical encoding.\n5. **Message envelope, register snapshot fields, and compiled vs. service division ([`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.3.4, [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.1):** Each service request carries the materialized register execution snapshot (`pc`/`registers`/`frames`/`K`/`store-of`/`value`/`halted`/`blocked` plus layout generation) before any service that can collect or park. External continuation export resolves tag-`13` arena references to the payload values in §3.5 or refuses. Pure body control, register moves, and guest closure calls stay compiled in the emitted wasm module; the host service is never allowed to “run the remaining program” to manufacture the expected trace.\n\nThe host-side implementation of `yin.vm.engine.host.v1`, the **single-engine-port rule**, and the\n“one transition implementation, two dispatchers” rule are specified in\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.4.1 and §E.4.2.\ndeclarations and expected refusals. ABI-specific probes extend K0 as a\ntransport test group without replacing format-level fixtures. Rust interpreter\ncoverage advances with each W slice; it cannot be omitted because native wasm\nhappens to pass. B0 results and complete boundary traces remain shared; wasm\nsingle-step traces add finer evidence, not a second oracle.\n\nBuild an explicit differential adapter rather than replacing the existing\ntests' private `register-run` vars by global mutation. Parameterize/extract\nshared fixtures when implementation is authorized. Each case receives\nindependent scripted streams with identical inputs, capability secret,\nprimitive registry, origin and counter setup. From W1 onward, compile each\nimage once and run those identical wasm bytes under **Node and Wasmtime**,\neach against interpreted register after every guest instruction and scheduler\nevent; Wasmer must also pass the complete suite by W5. Compare serialized ABI\ntraffic as well as full semantic traces; also compare semantic execution where\nthe existing A→R fragment permits it.\n\nThe generic per-fixture procedure, applied to every lane against the oracle\nexports: project the guest's admitted source to its canonical form and assert the\nliteral goldens; derive and validate the lowerings; compile and instantiate per\nlane; execute with the deterministic declared bindings (the guest specification\nnames them) and the genuine parked targets its resume cases require; execute every\nconditional arm where applicable; and compare the same qualified refusal where a\ncase is unsupported. Never silently skip resume, terminal, or closure cases, and\nnever relabel unsupported cases as successes. B0 normalization alone is\ninsufficient for backend acceptance: the guest specification's raw observable\nfields are compared in addition, as enumerated by the guest fixture specification\n(for the register machine, [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md)\n§10.4). Compare resources by\nauthenticated logical identity/cursor progress and stream emissions, not by\nhost object address. With identical secrets and inputs,\nhash/owner/seal/profile/address strings, guest-visible ids and slice ordering\nmust be bit-identical. Compare canonical expanded observable-state encodings,\nnot pointer layouts; private arena numbers may be renamed only where they\ncannot affect key ordering, id minting or guest state. Across independent\ntasks use the same explicit owner inputs; never erase owner checks. Test\nprimitive implementations and scripted transports exist in both native and\nCLJS compositions with the same declared profiles; a JS function fixture must\nget an equivalent native fixture, not disappear from the native run. Normalize\nonly private memory addresses, arena numbering and runtime object identities;\npreserve all guest values, errors, `pc`s, frames, snapshots, queues, counters,\nstore/heap/custody changes, resource progress and emitted request order.\nCompare failures by qualified rule/reason/outcome and relevant message\nassertions.\n\nThe planned LLVM lane uses this same kit under Rust §7.2: interpreted and\nnative runs compare boundary states, ordered emissions and refusal\ndiagnostics. Artifact-drop fallback continues interpreted from the same\nboundary. Wasm adds wasm→Rust-interpreter and wasm→CLJS-interpreter fallback\nfixtures. No backend forks the validator, live-set rules or payload\nvocabulary. K3 may measure wasm on the same harness; a wasm result does not\ncommission K4.\n\n#### E.5.2 Cross-embedder encoding, hash, and traversal determinism gates\n\nAcross every embedder (Node/CLJS, Wasmtime, Wasmer) and the shared Rust\ncrates, the conformance vectors enforce the three encoding domains (§E.2.2),\nthe private `yin.vm.engine.host.v1` ABI (§E.3), and the shared deterministic\ntraversal prerequisite (§E.4.3):\n### 10.4 Register conformance fixture schema (K0/W0)\n\n**W0 and Rust K0 are the same conformance-kit deliverable**, exported from R4,\nnot independent harness authorities\n(`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1185–1199`). The guest-agnostic differential\nconformance method (oracle export discipline, the CLJS/Rust-interpreter/wasm/LLVM\nlanes, trace-not-just-values comparison, B0-normalized vs. raw boundary\ncomparison, and determinism/snapshot gates) is specified in\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.1–§E.5.3.\n\nFor the register format and kernel, the K0/W0 kit exports, per corpus program:\n- the register image as canonical data,\n- `R` (`register-hash`),\n- the B0-normalized result or error, and\n- the format-level trace R4 produces (sparse continuation payload at every boundary, `K` depth, saved register pairs).\n\nPlus the required register fixture groups:\n- refusal fixtures for all fourteen validator rules with rule keywords,\n- the descriptor hash (`descriptor-hash`),\n- executable register scalar-encoding vectors (`yin-scalar` / `debruijn_code.cljc`),\n- diagnostic message fixtures,\n- refused-step atomicity fixtures (a refused step leaves the state equal),\n- tail-site fixtures for every row of the Rust design's §7.3 call-outcome table (including a tail primitive value and a tail primitive effect returning into a caller in another body, and halting with `K` empty; `docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:833–918`),\n- a wide-body fixture whose register count exceeds `65,535`,\n- `:host-limit` refusal fixtures, and\n- the million-iteration tail fixtures.\n\nRequired repository corpora for register-wasm and Rust K0–K5 (each processed by the\ngeneric projection/admission/execution/comparison procedure of\n[`yin.vm.engine.md`](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5.1; only the register-specific\nfixtures, bindings, and compared fields are listed here):\n- `test/yin/vm/semantic/corpus.cljc` **programs and literal goldens**: these goldens specify syntax/address, not a runnable environment or expected final value for every entry. Supply deterministic `f`/`g`/`h`/`k`/`x`/`y`/`c`/`loop` bindings, stream fixtures, and genuine parked targets for resume cases; otherwise compare the same qualified refusal.\n- All fixtures and assertions of `test/yin/vm/debruijn/register_test.cljc` (plus `test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc` and `test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code_test.cljc`), including hand images, B0/B2 parity, stream scripts, continuation corruption, and `IVM` behavior. The register raw observable fields compared beyond the method's B0-normalized values: register payload shapes, `store`/`module-stores`, `pc`, frame order, `K`, counter, flags, `wait-set`/`ready-queue`/`parked` entries, and emitted request traces.\n\n### 10.5 Register-specific W0–W6 implementation slices and acceptance gates\n\nGuest-agnostic W0 toolchain pins (`Node 25.6.1`, `Clojure CLI 1.12.4.1602 /\nshadow-cljs 3.3.5`, `WABT 1.0.37`, `Wasmtime =38.0.0`, `Wasmer 6.0.1`, `Rust /\n326:| `:return rs` | Read value. If K nonempty, pop frame, nil-initialize caller register vector, restore saved pairs and dest, frames and store-of, set value and return-pc; TRANSFER. Else set only value, halted true, store-of nil: keep pc at the executing return instruction and preserve active registers, frames, empty K and all other fields. The return-result restore case first changes pc as specified in [`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`](./yin.vm.debruijn.register.md) §10.2.2 (r5 §3.2). |\n| E | `3334657969bea328a8ce624103aeb1ec42dfc69273972013ff3b8007a034578a` |\n| R | `6d8901c8c7bcbd5cc3cf6708827f07667c2873da4e7b6f33199f149d17c799e1` |\n| Y | `afab172bb5205606a833ffe176bdfd88c40e6f77238ace5c9cc6dca619a0ca64` |\n| K | `c9b87be54b3bef13950aaf42e8250fcd3642aa6dfd7575255c3114f8562cac01` |\n\nRequired corrections are organizational and referential: replace Y's copied protocol definitions with engine references (F1), make the fixture/method boundary single-home without dropping register facts (F2), and fix the return row's r5 section citation (F3). No source/test changes or redesign are requested. Recheck these focused edits and the link/coverage invariants before committing.\n\nFAIL\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport re,hashlib\nfiles=[Path('docs/design')/x for x in ['yin.vm.engine.md','yin.vm.debruijn.register.md','yang.wasm.md','yin.vm.rust-kernel.md']]\nfor p in files:\n print(p.name,len(p.read_text().splitlines()),hashlib.sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest())\nassert hashlib.sha256(files[3].read_bytes()).hexdigest()=='c9b87be54b3bef13950aaf42e8250fcd3642aa6dfd7575255c3114f8562cac01'\nbad=[]; retired=[]\nfor p in Path('docs/design').glob('*.md'):\n t=p.read_text()\n for pat in [r'\\\\]\\\\(([\"'^'\"\\\\n)]+)\\\\)',r'\"'^'\"\\\\s{0,3}\\\\[[\"'^'\"\\\\]]+\\\\]:\\\\s*(\\\\S+)']:\n  for m in re.finditer(pat,t,re.M):\n   target=m[1].strip('<>').split('#')[0].split('?')[0]\n   if any(n in target for n in ['yin.vm.register-wasm.md','yin.vm.conformance-kit.md']): retired.append((p.name,target))\n   if p in files and target and not re.match(r'[a-z]+:',target) and not (p.parent/target).exists():bad.append((p.name,target))\nprint('Missing local Markdown file targets in survivors:',bad)\nprint('Retired Markdown targets across design docs:',retired)\nassert not bad and not retired\nfor needle in ['0x31524e59','| Control offset (bytes)','| Tag | Payload (no inner count)']:\n print(needle,[(p.name,t.count(needle)) for p in files if needle in (t:=p.read_text())])\ny=files[2].read_text(); r=files[1].read_text(); e=files[0].read_text()\nprint('opcode rows',len(re.findall(r'\"'^'\"\\\\| \"'`:'\"',y,re.M)))\nfor i in range(7): assert re.search(rf'\"'^#### 10'\"\\\\.5\\\\.\\\\d+ W{i} ',r,re.M)\nprint('W0–W6 present; Rust file byte-identical to round1.')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
